This assumes Claude Code deduplicates identical hook commands across the user-level and project-level `settings.json` scopes so a repo with both installs only fires each hook once. Claude Code's documented hook-merge behavior combines (concatenates) hooks from all applicable scopes rather than deduplicating identical command strings, and matching hooks run in parallel — so a repo that has both `entire enable` (repo-level) and `entire enable --global` (user-level) active would likely fire every Entire hook (session-start, stop, pre/post-task, etc.) twice per event, risking duplicate or racing writes to the shadow branch/session state. Please verify this assumption against actual Claude Code behavior before relying on it.

